-- title:
--   Node maximal ideal generator has valuation below one
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a positive level $N$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ which is perfect, and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$. Assume given the data needed to form a place specialisation — modular polynomial data for $q$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j, j_q)$), a proof $hKr$ that its bivariate reduction mod $q$ factors as $(C(X)^q - X)(C(X) - X^q)$, and the hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ that the Hecke homomorphisms $\overline{\alpha}, \overline{\beta}$ at level $N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are integral — together with a place specialisation $P$ and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$; these are summarised here. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$, let $w$ be a place of the characteristic-$q$ modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ N$ over $k$, and let $c$ be a pair of node coordinates $x, y$ in the node ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$ (the elements of $R$'s node integers whose Laurent expansion lies in the $K$-rational subfield at level $Nq$), subject to the conditions that the first residue of $x$ and the second residue of $y$ vanish, that the second residue of $x$ has order $1$ at the Frobenius translate $\mathrm{arithFrobC}\ q\ k\ N \cdot w$, and that the first residue of $y$ has order $1$ at $w$. Let $\varpi$ belong to $A \cap K$ (the coefficient subring), assume the node ring is local, and assume its maximal ideal equals the ideal generated by the constant $\varpi$, by $x$ and by $y$. Then the $A$-valuation of $\varpi$, viewed in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is strictly less than $1$.
--
--   The statement records that, once the local ring of the node is presented with maximal ideal $(\varpi, x, y)$, the coefficient element $\varpi$ is a genuine non-unit of $A$, so that $A$ is a non-trivial valuation ring and $\varpi$ can serve as a uniformising parameter in the vertical direction. It is used as a preliminary normalisation in the counting estimates at supersingular nodes, for instance in the bounds on lengths and ranks of quotients of the node ring proved downstream.
